C++ Relational Operator Overloading
Description

This C++ program demonstrates relational operator overloading by overloading the > operator for a Distance class.

The overloaded > operator is used to compare the meter values of two Distance objects and determine which distance is greater.

Class Used
1. Distance Class

The Distance class is used to store and compare distances.

meters stores the distance in meters.
The constructor initializes the distance value.
operator>() overloads the greater-than (>) operator.
display() displays the distance in meters.
operator>() Function

The operator>() function compares the meters values of two Distance objects.

It returns:

true if the first distance is greater.
false if the first distance is not greater.

For example:

120 meters > 90 meters
        ↓
      true
Main Function

In the main() function, two Distance objects are created:

First distance: 120 meters
Second distance: 90 meters

The > operator is then used to compare the two objects.

If the first distance is greater, the program displays an appropriate message.

Output
First distance: 120 meters
Second distance: 90 meters
First distance is greater
OOP Concept Used

Relational Operator Overloading

       Distance
       /      \
   First     Second
 120 m        90 m
      \      /
       > operator
           ↓
         true

The expression:

first > second

uses the overloaded operator>() function to compare the two Distance objects.